# 2. Core Design Principle

The system should distinguish between:

### Patient lifecycle

```text
Registration
     ↓
Screening / Clinical Assessment
     ↓
Diagnosis / Eligibility Assessment
     ↓
Enrollment where applicable
     ↓
Follow-up
     ↓
Outcome / Continuing Care
```

### Clinical encounter lifecycle

```text
Appointment / Arrival
        ↓
Check-in
        ↓
Triage
        ↓
Clinical Assessment
        ↓
Orders / Requests
        ↓
Laboratory / Diagnostics / Pharmacy
        ↓
Treatment / Management
        ↓
Encounter Completion
```

### Research lifecycle

```text
Study
 ↓
Screening
 ↓
Eligibility
 ↓
Enrollment
 ↓
Study Visits
 ↓
CRFs / Clinical Services
 ↓
Follow-up
 ↓
Study Outcome
```

These workflows interact but should not be unnecessarily merged into one linear process.

---

# 3. Patient as the Central Entity

The **Patient** is the central clinical entity.

A patient can:

* Receive normal hospital care
* Be screened for an NCD
* Have one or more NCD conditions
* Attend scheduled follow-up
* Attend unscheduled visits
* Be enrolled in a research study
* Participate in multiple studies where permitted
* Receive laboratory services
* Receive imaging
* Have ECG/Echo
* Receive medication
* Be admitted
* Be referred
* Have clinical outcomes

The system should maintain one longitudinal patient record.

```text
PATIENT
 │
 ├── Registrations
 ├── Appointments
 ├── Encounters
 ├── Triage
 ├── Diagnoses
 ├── Laboratory
 ├── Radiology
 ├── ECG
 ├── Echocardiography
 ├── Prescriptions
 ├── Pharmacy
 ├── NCD Programs
 └── Research Studies
```

---

# 4. Hospital/Clinical Management Module

The general clinical system should contain the following major modules.

## 4.1 Patient Management

Functions:

* Patient registration
* Patient identification
* Demographics
* Contact information
* Address
* Next of kin
* Identification documents
* Patient search
* Duplicate detection
* Patient merging according to permissions
* Patient status

---

# 5. Appointment Management

Appointments should support:

* New appointment
* Follow-up appointment
* Specialist appointment
* Diagnostic appointment
* Laboratory appointment
* Pharmacy-related appointment
* Study visit appointment
* NCD follow-up appointment

Appointment statuses:

```text
Scheduled
Confirmed
Checked In
Completed
Cancelled
Rescheduled
No Show
```

Appointments are not the same as encounters.

A patient may have an appointment but never attend.

---

# 6. Encounter Management

Every actual facility visit should be represented by an **Encounter**.

Examples:

```text
Scheduled Clinical Encounter
Unscheduled Clinical Encounter
Emergency Encounter
Outpatient Encounter
Inpatient Encounter
Research-related Encounter
NCD Follow-up Encounter
Diagnostic Encounter
```

The encounter provides the context for clinical activities.

```text
PATIENT
   ↓
ENCOUNTER
   ↓
Triage
   ↓
Clinical Assessment
   ↓
Orders
   ↓
Services
   ↓
Treatment
```

---

# 7. Triage Module

Triage should be encounter-based.

Typical information may include:

* Blood pressure
* Pulse
* Respiratory rate
* Temperature
* Oxygen saturation
* Weight
* Height
* BMI
* Pain score
* Blood glucose where applicable
* Other configurable measurements

The exact measurements should be configurable by clinical context.

For example, NCD screening may require additional measurements compared with a routine encounter.

---

# 8. Clinical Consultation

The consultation module should capture:

* Chief complaint
* History
* Symptoms
* Clinical examination
* Assessment
* Diagnosis
* Differential diagnosis where required
* Treatment plan
* Referral
* Follow-up plan
* Clinical notes

Clinical consultation should not contain every laboratory or pharmacy field.

Instead, it creates requests to specialized modules.

---

# 9. Clinical Orders / Requests

A common order/request mechanism should connect clinical encounters to specialized services.

```text
Encounter
   │
   ├── Laboratory Request
   ├── Radiology Request
   ├── ECG Request
   ├── Echocardiography Request
   ├── Procedure Request
   └── Pharmacy Prescription
```

Each operational module then manages its own workflow.

---

# 10. Laboratory Module

Laboratory must be a separate operational module.

Basic workflow:

```text
Laboratory Request
       ↓
Specimen Collection
       ↓
Specimen Processing
       ↓
Testing
       ↓
Result Entry
       ↓
Verification
       ↓
Final Result
       ↓
Report
```

Potential features:

* Test catalog
* Panels
* Specimen types
* Specimen tracking
* Reference ranges
* Result entry
* Result verification
* Abnormal/critical results
* Laboratory reports
* Quality control
* Laboratory statistics

The research and NCD systems should request laboratory tests through this module rather than creating separate "research laboratory" systems.

---

# 11. Radiology / Imaging Module

Separate module for:

* X-ray
* Ultrasound
* CT
* MRI
* Other imaging procedures

Workflow:

```text
Imaging Request
      ↓
Scheduling
      ↓
Procedure
      ↓
Image/Study
      ↓
Radiologist/Clinician Report
      ↓
Verification
```

