# 21. Study Lifecycle

```text
Study
 ↓
Participant Screening
 ↓
Eligibility Assessment
 ↓
Eligible / Not Eligible
 ↓
Enrollment
 ↓
Visit Schedule
 ↓
Study Visits
 ↓
CRFs + Clinical Services
 ↓
Study Outcome
```

Eligibility must occur before enrollment.

---

# 22. Study Visits

A study visit should be separate from a generic clinical encounter.

A study visit represents a protocol-defined event such as:

```text
Screening
Baseline / Day 0
Day 7
Day 14
Month 1
Month 3
Month 6
Month 12
```

The study visit may generate one or more clinical encounters.

---

# 23. CRF Management

CRFs should not all be mandatory at every visit.

A configurable CRF system should support:

```text
Study
  ↓
Protocol
  ↓
Visit Type
  ↓
CRF Assignment
  ↓
CRF Response
```

For example:

```text
Baseline
├── Demographics
├── Medical History
├── Risk Factors
└── Baseline Assessment

Month 3
├── Clinical Assessment
└── Disease-specific CRF

Adverse Event
└── AE CRF
```

CRFs can be:

* Participant-level
* Enrollment-level
* Visit-level
* Encounter-level
* Event-level

---

# 24. Shared Clinical Services and Research

Research should reuse hospital clinical services.

For example:

```text
Research Study
     ↓
Study Visit
     ↓
Clinical Request
     ├── Laboratory
     ├── ECG
     ├── Echo
     ├── Radiology
     └── Pharmacy
```

There should not be unnecessary duplicate modules such as:

```text
Research Laboratory
Research ECG
Research Pharmacy
```

Instead, the normal clinical modules should support research context.

---

# 25. Follow-up Management Engine

The Follow-up Engine is a major shared component.

It should support both:

* NCD management
* Research studies

and potentially other chronic disease programs.

The engine should separate:

```text
Follow-up Plan
        ↓
Schedule Rule
        ↓
Scheduled Visit
        ↓
Clinical Encounter
```

An unscheduled encounter is independent of this chain.

---

# 26. Follow-up Frequency

The system should support:

* Daily
* Weekly
* Every N weeks
* Monthly
* Every N months
* Quarterly
* Yearly
* Custom schedule

Example:

```text
Every 1 month
Every 2 months
Every 3 months
Every 6 months
Every 12 months
```

---

# 27. Custom Visit Schedule

The system must also support irregular schedules.

Example:

```text
Baseline       Day 0
Visit 1        Day 7
Visit 2        Day 14
Visit 3        Day 30
Visit 4        Day 60
Visit 5        Day 90
Visit 6        Day 180
Visit 7        Day 365
```

This is particularly important for clinical research.

---

# 28. Follow-up Scheduling Mode

The system should provide three scheduling modes:

```text
Scheduling Mode
│
├── Automatic
├── Manual
└── Hybrid
```

---

# 29. Automatic Scheduling

In automatic mode, the system generates future visits according to the configured rules.

Example:

```text
Frequency: Every 3 months
Mode: Automatic
```

The system can generate:

```text
Baseline
   ↓
Month 3
   ↓
Month 6
   ↓
Month 9
   ↓
Month 12
```

---

# 30. Manual Scheduling

In manual mode, future visits are not automatically generated.

After an encounter, staff select:

**Schedule Next Visit**

and specify:

* Date
* Visit type
* Reason
* Provider/service
* Location
* Notes

The next visit is then created manually.

---

# 31. Hybrid Scheduling

Hybrid mode combines automatic generation with staff control.

The system may automatically create future visits while allowing authorized staff to:

* Change dates
* Reschedule
* Add extra visits
* Skip a visit
* Cancel a planned visit
* Change future frequency
* Create an unscheduled visit

This is particularly useful for real-world NCD management.

---

# 32. Automatic Generation Horizon

Automatic scheduling should allow staff to define **how far into the future visits should be generated**.

Example:

```text
Frequency: Every 3 months
Generate ahead: 12 months
```

Another configuration:

```text
Frequency: Every 1 month
Generate ahead: 3 months
```

If the current month is September:

```text
October   Generated
November  Generated
December  Generated
January   Not yet generated
```

This prevents the system from unnecessarily generating years of future visits.

---

# 33. Maximum Future Visits

Automatic scheduling should also support a maximum number of future visits.

Example:

```text
Frequency: Every 1 month
Generate ahead: 12 months
Maximum future visits: 3
```

The system generates only:

```text
October
November
December
```

When one visit is completed, the system can generate the next future visit.

This creates a **rolling schedule**.

---

# 34. Rolling Automatic Scheduling

Example:

```text
Maximum future visits = 3
```

Initially:

```text
October
November
December
```

October is completed.

The system then generates:

```text
November
December
January
```

November is completed:

```text
December
January
February
```

This keeps a controlled number of future visits available.

---

# 35. Follow-up Reference Point

Automatic scheduling must allow selection of the date from which future visits are calculated.

Options:

```text
Calculate future visits from:
├── Baseline
├── Last completed visit
├── Custom date
└── Custom month
```

---

# 36. Baseline Reference

Example:

```text
Baseline: 10 January 2027
Frequency: Every 3 months
```

Generated dates:

```text
10 April 2027
10 July 2027
10 October 2027
10 January 2028
```

The schedule remains anchored to baseline.

---

# 37. Last Completed Visit Reference

Example:

```text
Expected visit: 10 April
Actual completed visit: 18 April
```

If the reference rule is:

**Last completed visit**

the next visit is calculated from 18 April.

```text
18 April
   ↓ + 3 months
18 July
   ↓ + 3 months
18 October
```

---

# 38. Custom Reference

Staff may choose a custom date or month.

Example:

```text
Reference type: Custom
Reference date: 01 June 2027
Frequency: Every 3 months
```

The system generates:

```text
01 September 2027
01 December 2027
01 March 2028
```

---

# 39. Changing Follow-up Frequency

The system must allow future follow-up frequency to change.

Example:

```text
Every 3 months
       ↓
Clinical decision
       ↓
Every 1 month
```

Later:

```text
Every 1 month
       ↓
Condition stable
       ↓
Every 3 months
```

Changing the frequency should affect **future planned visits**, not historical completed visits.

---

# 40. Changing an Individual Visit

Staff should be able to change a future planned visit.

Example:

```text
Original:
15 December

New:
20 December
```

The system should record:

* Original date
* New date
* Reason
* User
* Timestamp

---

# 41. Future Visit Deletion

Future visits with no clinical data should be editable.

Example:

```text
✓ Baseline — Completed
○ Month 3 — Planned
○ Month 6 — Planned
○ Month 9 — Planned
```

Month 6 can be:

* Edited
* Rescheduled
* Cancelled
* Deleted

subject to user permissions.

---

# 42. Protection of Historical Data

Once a visit has clinical data, it should not normally be deleted.

Example:

```text
Month 3
├── Vitals
├── Laboratory
├── Consultation
├── Diagnosis
└── Medication
```

This visit becomes protected.

Instead of deleting it, the system should support:

* Correction
* Amendment
* Void/cancel with authorization
* Audit trail

---

# 43. Visit Statuses

Recommended statuses:

```text
Planned
Due
In Progress
Completed
Missed
Cancelled
Rescheduled
Not Applicable
```

Additional statuses can be introduced if required.

---

# 44. Unscheduled Visits

The system must support visits that are not part of the planned schedule.

Examples:

* New symptoms
* Complication
* Adverse event
* Emergency
* Medication problem
* Abnormal laboratory result
* Referral
* Walk-in
* Clinical review

```text
Unscheduled Encounter
       ↓
Triage
       ↓
Clinical Assessment
       ↓
Orders
       ↓
Services
       ↓
Treatment / Outcome
```

---

# 45. Unscheduled Research Visit

A research participant can have an unscheduled encounter without replacing the scheduled study visit.

Example:

```text
Scheduled:
Month 3 Study Visit

Additional:
Unscheduled Visit
Reason: New symptoms
```

Both records remain.

This is particularly important for adverse events and clinical safety monitoring.

---

# 46. Missed Visits

A scheduled visit may become:

```text
Missed
```

The system should allow authorized staff to:

* Record reason
* Contact participant
* Reschedule
* Mark as not applicable
* Record loss to follow-up where appropriate

A missed visit should not be confused with an unscheduled visit.

